About 5,000 years ago — the beginnings of organized archives. In Mesopotamia, around 3000 BCE, institutions were producing and retaining written tablets. By approximately 2400–2350 BCE, the royal palace at Ebla, in modern Syria, had a substantial archive of documents deliberately stored together. Archaeologists recovered roughly 15,000–17,000 tablets and fragments from its principal archive rooms.
The Text Message
+2
About 2,700 years ago — a famous example of deliberately collecting knowledge. The Assyrian king Ashurbanipal assembled a major collection at Nineveh, around 668–631 BCE. Its approximately 30,000 tablets included literary works, scholarly texts, dictionaries and other knowledge—not merely administrative accounts. It's one of the clearest ancient examples of an organized collection intended to preserve and consult writings.
Guinness World Records
+1
So my answer depends on your precise threshold:
An organized, deliberately retained archive: roughly 5,000 years ago.
A major curated collection of writings and knowledge: roughly 2,700 years ago.
Those are the earliest relevant milestones we can identify confidently, not proof that nobody did it earlier.
And I suspect you're getting at something deeper than the age of archives: the point at which human beings began making memory that could outlive the people who created it. 💜📚

